  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 1220 AM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nThe coldest night so far this season is expected Wednesday night,\nwith sub-freezing temperatures forecast across much of west\ncentral, southwest and north central NM. A Freeze Watch has been\nissued for the upper Rio Grande Valley, including Taos, for late\nWednesday night into Thursday, with a high probability for the 1st\nfreeze of the season in that area. The departing and weakening \nupper low will leave behind a baggy trough oriented from northeast\nto southwest across the region on Thursday, which is forecast to \nclose off into a 2nd upper low and retrograde southwest across AZ \nthrough Thursday night. At the same time, a backdoor cold front \nwill race west across NM, resulting in a strong east canyon/gap \nwind into the Rio Grande and Upper Tularosa Valleys. While the 00Z  \nMOS guidance has come down from some of the previous runs, there \nis a still a high probability of at least advisory-type speeds \nlate Thursday through Thursday night. Daytime temperatures will be\nwell below average on Friday behind the backdoor cold front. The \nweekend forecast is more uncertain, but the retrograding upper \nlow is forecast to stall over the Baja Peninsula and may pick up \nand steer tropical moisture north into NM, resulting in another \nround of precipitation.",
